    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm trying to dive a bit deeper into the specific \"instability\" aspect. When we see a rapid influx of certain ethnic or immigrant groups into an area—say, groups with high concentrations from specific regions like Central America or South Asia—does that change the risk profile compared to, say, a demographic shift toward a more high-income European immigrant group? I'm trying to understand if the cultural or demographic makeup itself carries weight in your models for predicting social friction or crime spikes.",
